# Sixteen tasks answer neither signal

**DONE.** Every task file now carries a status word and the check
reports no mismatch. What it found is below, and two of the sixteen
changed state.

Before this, the agreement check in `CLAUDE.md` reported a mismatch for
51 of the task files, and every one of them for the same reason: the file
carries no status word at all. The check cannot tell a done task from
an open one, so it says "open" and the `.done` suffix disagrees.

## What the check sees today

    51 files carry no **OPEN/DONE/...** word in their first 12 lines.
    35 of those are named `*.md.done`, so the suffix answers and only
       the check is noisy.
    16 are named `*.md`, so NOTHING answers.

The sixteen:

    008 transitive-policy              029 typed-dicts-for-the-protocol
    012 test-blind-spots               032 log-callbacks-to-the-client
    014 transport-shims                033 primops-in-python
    015 real-nix-spike                 034 functions-as-values
    016 evaluation-server              035 anyio-not-asyncio
    022 proto-field-stability          037 tests-outside-the-sandbox
    025 wrap-only-what-needs-wrapping  040 store-paths-as-filesystem-paths
    026 typed-proxy-parameters         050 shim-methods-hand-type-their-signatures

## Why it matters

`tasks/031` was named as the next thing to do, twice, from a summary
that read its filename and not its text. It was closed - the refusal
it described is the boundary it deliberately landed. That cost two
wrong recommendations, and the only thing that would have prevented it
is the file saying so on line one.

The sixteen above are that same trap, sixteen times over. Some of them
are certainly finished: `035 anyio-not-asyncio` names a decision the
suite already runs under, and `037 tests-outside-the-sandbox` names the
split `default.nix` already makes.

## What the reading found

Fourteen stay OPEN, and each now says in one sentence what is left.
Three of those were worth checking rather than assuming:

- **035** (anyio, not asyncio). The suite runs under anyio and the
  runtime does not. `payload/runtime.py` calls `loop.run_in_executor`
  in four places, which is exactly the half the file calls movable
  now. Read as done from the suite alone; it is not.
- **022** (proto field stability). No lockfile, and nothing reads the
  manifest's `"schema": 1`. Field numbers are still positional.
- **040** (store paths as filesystem paths). `decl/path.py` has no
  `__fspath__` and no pathlib construction.

Two changed state:

- **015** (real-Nix spike) is **DONE**. Its question was whether this
  could bind real libstore, and every declaration in the repo now
  names a real header. What the file records afterwards - what the
  real methods are NOT tested on - is handed to `tasks/037`.
- **050** (shim methods hand-type their signatures) is **CLOSED**. Its
  premise is gone: it argues about hand-typed `.pxd` shims and
  Cython's pure mode, and there is no `.pyx` or `.pxd` in the repo.
  A nanobind binding is emitted whole from a declaration, so the
  duplication it complains about cannot recur in the shape it names.

**034** was the one that looked closed and is not. `tasks/015` records
Carl aborting it - "maybe it's better if we abort 034 and start linking
against real Nix" - but that aborted building function values into the
MOCK. Linking real Nix removed the reason it was blocked, not the work.

The 35 `.done` files got the word too, in one line that says the suffix
is the record and this is not a new judgement. The suffix already
answered; the check reads both places, so both have to hold something.

## What NOT to do

Do not change the check to ignore a missing word. It is reporting a
real gap, and a check taught to be quiet about it would leave the
sixteen exactly as they are while looking clean.

Opened and closed 2026-09-02, after closing `tasks/075`.
